🤖 [Qwen Edge] In Java, there isn't a single "standard" way to reverse a string because different situations may require slightly different approaches depending on whether you're working with strings or arrays of characters.

However, if you want a clean and concise solution that also works for both strings (which are essentially sequences of characters) and character arrays, the standard method uses `StringBuilder` class. This is useful when dealing with potentially long strings where performance might be an issue compared to simple array manipulation methods like reversing using two pointers or by appending in reverse order.

Here's how you can do it:

```java
public String reverseString(String input) {
    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder(input);
    return result.reverse().toString();
}
```

This method is efficient and works well for any string, regardless of its length. It leverages the `StringBuilder` class which is designed for this kind of operation.

If you're working with an actual character array (which in Java would usually be represented as a byte array or char array), you can reverse it like so:

```java
public String reverseCharArray(char[] input) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (int i = 0; i < input.length; i++) {
        sb.append(input[input.length - 1 - i]);
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

This method simply appends characters from the end of the array to a `StringBuilder`, effectively reversing it.

Both methods are idiomatic in Java and adhere to best practices for string manipulation.

