```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	// URL to the resource we want to fetch
	url := "https://api.example.com/data"

	// Create a new HTTP client instance
	client := &http.Client{}

	// Define a function that will handle the response and print it
	responseHandler := func(resp *http.Response, err error) {
		if err != nil {
			fmt.Println("Error fetching data:", err)
		} else {
			defer resp.Body.Close()

			// Check if we have content (not empty)
			if resp.StatusCode == http.StatusOK && len(respBody) > 0 {
				bodyString := make([]byte, len(respBody))
				copy(bodyString, respBody)
				fmt.Println("Response received successfully:", string(bodyString))
			} else {
				fmt.Printf("Status code: %d\n", resp.StatusCode)
			}
		}
	}

	// Perform the GET request
	resp, err := client.Get(url)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error making GET request:", err)
		return
	}

	// Call the response handler with the response and error (if any)
	responseHandler(resp, err)

	// Print status code of the response
	fmt.Printf("Response Status Code: %d\n", resp.StatusCode)
}
```

This example shows how to create a new `http.Client` instance for making HTTP requests. Then, you define a function that handles both the response and error cases when processing the response.

After calling the `client.Get()` method, it returns an `*http.Response` object representing the result of the request. You can pass this `resp` to your handler function defined earlier or use other methods available on the `Response` type such as reading its body content if needed.

Here's what each part does:
- We define a URL that we want to access.
- We create an HTTP client instance which is used for making requests.
- The `responseHandler` function handles both response and error cases of a request by printing appropriate messages or extracting the body if successful.
- Finally, you call this handler with the actual response and the associated error (if any).

